2. Mechanical-edit policy
-------------------------

Only three kinds of edit are allowed to any file in `kernel/`, and no others:

  (A) include replacement -- `#include "server.h"` / `"zmalloc.h"` /
      `"redisassert.h"` / `"atomicvar.h"` / `"object.h"` become
      `#include "redislib_port.h"`; the replacement is 1:1, so a file that
      included three now-shimmed headers gets three `redislib_port.h` lines
      (harmless, they are guarded, and it keeps the diff line-symmetric).
  (B) command-layer removal -- deleting `*Command(client *c, ...)` functions and
      the static helpers that only they called, plus the `client`/`obj` reply
      plumbing those functions used. Nothing else may be deleted.
  (C) one port-notice line added directly below the copyright block, reading
      either "command layer removed", "include shim applied (redislib_port.h)",
      or "copied verbatim from Redis 8.10.0", each ending with
      "see redislib-src/NOTICE".

Algorithm lines are never re-indented, rewritten, "improved" or reordered. This
is machine-checked: `git diff` of any kernel file against the matching `$RSRC`
file must show only (A), (B) and (C) shapes. As of Task 1 the audit produced
zero non-mechanical added lines in all 40 ported files; removals are confined to
`bitops.c` (1062 lines), `hyperloglog.c` (522) and `t_zset.c` (2302), which are
the three files that carried command functions. Kept deliberately even though
nothing calls them yet: the `zsl*` / `zzl*` skiplist and listpack API (the plan
requires all of it) and `invalid_hll_err` in `hyperloglog.c` (its text is the
PF* error message Task 3 must reproduce).

`redislib_port.h`, `redislib_port.c` and `t_zset.h` are the only files not taken
from Redis: the first two are the shim described in section 4, and `t_zset.h` is
the sorted-set / skiplist type block plus the `zsl*`/`zzl*` prototypes copied out
of `$RSRC/src/server.h` (which is not part of the port). `kernel/` holds 43 files
in total: 20 `.c` plus 23 `.h`, of which 40 come from Redis.


