5. Verification
---------------

Line-ending note for every byte comparison below: the project default is
`* text=auto`, which would hand a Windows checkout CRLF files while `$RSRC` is
LF. `.gitattributes` pins `redislib-src/**` and the two redislib test files to
`eol=lf` so the comparison works in any checkout; if a comparison ever reports
*every* line changed, that is a line-ending artefact -- strip the CR
(`sed 's/\r$//'`) before believing it.

`tools/redislib-harness.c` is a pure-C assertion program over the ported kernels
(geohash encode/decode round trip and the distance helpers, HLL 10k cardinality
inside +/-2% plus sparse->dense and hllMerge, dict insert/rehash/lookup/delete
with siphash, sds, util string<->number, intset encoding upgrades, adlist,
bitops popcount/bitpos/bitfield/overflow, and the zset kernel: skiplist add/
update/conditions/del, all rank orderings, listpack<->skiplist conversion
round trip, zsetDup, direct zsl API). It ends with:

    redislib harness OK: hll=9973

Build and run it with a native Linux gcc from the Windows side:

    MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1 MSYS2_ARG_CONV_EXCL='*' \
      wsl -d Ubuntu-24.04 -- bash /mnt/e/git/skynetjit/tools/run-harness-wsl.sh

The runner compiles every `.c` in `kernel/` (all 21, including the newly added
listpack.c) plus the harness. gcc 13.3.0 build is warning-clean apart from 8
"defined but not used" notices for the static `zsl*`/`zzl*`/`zdiff` helpers kept
on purpose (section 2). `-Wno-unused-variable` and `-Wno-maybe-uninitialized`
are passed by the runner; see its header comment for the reasons.

`hll=9973` is the expected estimate for 10000 distinct elements at the default
HLL precision (Redis' own standard error is 0.81%), and it is deterministic
because the HLL hash is siphash with a randomised dict seed only for `dict`, not
for HLL.

Runtime side, per facade task: `tests/redislib_smoke.lua` (started by
`tests/redislib-config.lua` inside the built tree) drives the real Lua modules
through skynet -- bitmap since Task 2, hll since Task 3, each asserting the
Redis reply shapes and the upstream error texts. It is green on both platforms:
`build.sh` on Windows and `build-linux.sh` under WSL Ubuntu 24.04 print
`redislib-smoke: redislib modules succeeded`.

Scope note: the harness is a Linux-native-gcc program (WSL Ubuntu 24.04, gcc
13.3.0).

Windows (MinGW-w64 gcc 16) compiles the whole kernel and both shipped facades
with zero warnings now. The four portability problems an early per-file syntax
scan reported are fixed at shim/flag level, and none of them touched an
algorithm line:

    sha256.h  `typedef uint32_t WORD` collides with windows.h' WORD
              -> guarded with `#ifndef _WINDEF_` (section 4)
    util.c    mkdir(dname, 0755)  -- MSVCRT declares mkdir with one argument
    util.c    redis_fsync(fd)     -- no fsync(), _commit() is the equivalent
              -> the `#if defined(_WIN32)` mapping block in redislib_port.h
    RL_CFLAGS carried the repo's WIN_FORCE (compat.h -> windows.h), which is what
              produced the WORD clash in the first place -> removed from
              RL_CFLAGS; the kernel is plain C and needs no Lua compat headers

Two more warning classes are suppressed for the kernel objects only, by
`integration/redislib.mk`; the facade rule keeps the full `-Wall`:

    -Wno-pointer-to-int-cast  LLP64 (`long` is 32-bit) warns on upstream's
              pointer casts -- the alignment masks in bitops.c, the pointer terms
              of dictFingerprint() in dict.c, lpRepr() in listpack.c. Each keeps
              the bits it masks or feeds a debug-only fingerprint, so no
              behaviour depends on the width; rewriting them would put
              non-upstream casts inside verbatim kernel files.
    -Wno-unused-variable      file-scope symbols whose only consumers were the
              command layer deleted by edit B, e.g. hyperloglog.c's
              invalid_hll_err (the facades re-declare the text).


Geo verification (Task 5): the ten GEO* commands of spec §3.3 are in the same
smoke test, on the data set of Redis' own GEO documentation (Palermo / Catania),
so the distances and coordinates it asserts are Redis' published replies:
`geodist` = 166274.1516 m and its km/mi/ft quotients, and from (15,37) Catania
is 56.4413 km away with Palermo 190.4424 km, in ASC order, to 1e-4. The
anti-meridian case asserts Redis' own asymmetry (see §6) instead of a hoped-for
behaviour.

Sorted-set specific verification (Task 4): all 31 commands of spec §3.3 run in
`tests/redislib_smoke.lua`, on both encodings (the facade switches to the
skiplist past `zset_max_listpack_entries` on its own, and the test asserts both
branches). The performance self check of the plan is a soft indicator, recorded
here rather than asserted, because it is machine dependent: MinGW-w64 build,
1e6 `zadd` + `zrange(0,100)` + a full WITHSCORES scan = 1.27 s total (the plan's
limit was 10 s), `zcount`/`zrank` over 1e6 elements are sub-millisecond (they
take the O(log N) `zslNthInRange()` path, as Redis does), and
`zremrangebyscore()` over 500k elements is 0.23 s.

6. Deviations from a live Redis, by command family
---------------------------------------------------

Every one of these is also marked `rl:` at its site in the facade source. They
are the complete list of places where a redislib command cannot be byte-for-byte
the same program as the Redis command, and they are what a reviewer should
compare against `$RSRC` instead of expecting equality.

Object model (all families): there is no keyspace, no client, no DB and no
protocol. A command's key argument becomes the handle the method is called on,
`addReply*` becomes the Lua reply (spec §3.2), `server.dirty` /
`notifyKeyspaceEvent` / AOF-and-replica propagation are absent, and an object
that empties out stays addressable instead of being deleted (so `zcard()` after
`zrem()`-ing everything replies 0 where a live server replies 0 for a missing
key too, and `zadd()` after that starts from empty in both).

Bitmap: none beyond the object model. BITFIELD's offset cap is Redis' own
`proto-max-bulk-len` default (512MB), taken as a constant because there is no
config to read.

HyperLogLog: PFDEBUG SIMD has no runtime switch (this build defines neither
HAVE_AVX2 nor HAVE_AARCH64_NEON), so it always answers "disabled", which is what
a scalar Redis build answers; and a bad ON/OFF argument raises instead of
replying the error and then the status the way upstream's fall-through does,
because one Lua call returns one value. PFCOUNT's cached-cardinality write-back
does not mark anything dirty. `createHLLObject()`'s robj is a view the facade
discards; kernel calls that may replace `o->ptr` are handed a stack robj and the
sds is written back after every call.

Sorted set: range bounds are parsed by the facade with the same
`fast_float_strtod()` + rejection rules as the file-local `zslParseRange()`
(which is why a score of "1e400" is refused while a bound of "1e400" is not --
upstream uses `string2d()` for scores and `fast_float_strtod()` for bounds);
range *deletion* walks the range and calls `zsetDel()` per member instead of the
file-local `zsl/zzlDeleteRangeBy*()` helpers, so it is O(M log N) rather than
O(M + log N) on a skiplist; ZDIFF has only upstream's algorithm 2, not the
1-versus-2 cost heuristic; ZMPOP's reply carries the source handle where Redis
carries the key name, and ZUNION/ZINTER/ZDIFF/ZINTERCARD take their key list as
one array table (Redis' `numkeys` integer has nothing to count here).
Scores are Lua numbers in both directions (spec §3.1's type principle); on the
wire Redis sends a double as a bulk string, so `tools/redis-diff` compares them
numerically.

Geo: `GEOADD` reads CH itself (upstream leaves CH to the `zaddCommand()` it
re-dispatches to, and there is no re-dispatch here); the option words are still
accepted in the same positions, and the reply rule is upstream's -- `CH` makes
it count updates as well as additions. `STORE` / `STOREDIST` name a handle
instead of a key, and that handle may be a geoset or a plain sorted set, which
is the type Redis writes there. `WITHHASH` truncates to an integer like
`addReplyLongLong()`, so with a STOREDIST-written target (whose scores are
distances, not geohashes) it reports the truncated distance, exactly as the
server does. Distances are the value Redis *renders* -- four decimals, then read
back as a number -- not the full-precision double, so a client switching between
a real server and this library sees the same quantity.
Searching from exactly +180 longitude finds only the points east of the
antimeridian, even with an 5000 km radius: `geohashCalculateAreasByShapeWGS84()`
saturates the box at +180 and the neighbour boxes never wrap. That is upstream's
behaviour (the kernel is verbatim), not the port's, and the smoke test asserts
it on purpose: a search from -180, and one from a member at -179.99, do find
both sides.

Dump and load (spec §4; not Redis commands, so this is the one place where a
redislib value crosses a process boundary): a bitmap's or an HLL's `dump()` is
the Redis *string value* itself -- byte for byte what `GET` of such a key
returns, sparse or dense -- and `load()` is `SET` plus, for an HLL, the same
read-in check every PF* command makes (`isHLLObjectOrReply()`'s data side, so a
blob that is too short, mis-magicked, has an unknown encoding byte, or is dense
with the wrong length gets `WRONGTYPE Key is not a valid HyperLogLog string
value.`). Because PFCOUNT caches its estimate inside the value, as the server
does, a `dump()` taken after a count differs by those 8 bytes from one taken
before it -- the same two sketches converge once both have been counted, which
the smoke test asserts. The sorted-set family uses the small non-RDB layout
spec §4 defines (`uint32 count + (float64 score, uint32 len, bytes member)*`,
integers written with shifts so the blob is little-endian on any host, records
in ascending score order so one set always writes one blob). It is deliberately
not Redis' DUMP format, which is RDB plus a CRC64 trailer and is not what "a
skynet service can store this in any medium" needs; a blob is replayed against a
real server with ZADD / GEOADD instead, and the smoke test hand-decodes one to
prove no reader needs a Redis library. Loading validates the shape strictly --
a count that cannot be filled from the remaining bytes is refused *before* the
dictionary is sized from it, and trailing bytes, truncation, and a NaN score all
raise `Bad data format`, upstream's own reply for a serialized payload it cannot
parse (`cluster.c:290`). `+inf` and `-inf` are legal scores there because ZADD
accepts them.

7. Error replies: the code a client sees
-----------------------------------------

`rl_error()` (facade/rl_common.c) is the port of `addReplyErrorLength()`
(networking.c:657), and it is the only place that decides how an error reads:

    if (!len || s[0] != '-') addReplyProto(c,"-ERR ",5);

Upstream's rule is that a message carrying its own error code writes the code
into the string with a leading dash -- `"-WRONGTYPE Key is not a valid
HyperLogLog string value."` (hyperloglog.c:1636), `"-INVALIDOBJ Corrupted HLL
object detected"` (hyperloglog.c:208), `shared.syntaxerr` = `"-ERR syntax
error\r\n"` (server.c:2225) -- and every other message gets the generic code.
So the text a client actually reads is `WRONGTYPE ...` for the first group and
`ERR syntax error`, `ERR need numeric radius`, `ERR value is not a valid float`
for the second.

The facade call sites keep upstream's literals *including* the leading dash, and
`rl_error()` applies the one rule, so a Lua caller of any redislib command sees
exactly the text a Redis client sees for the same failure -- which is what makes
switching a call site from `skynet.db.redis` to these modules a non-event for
error handling. Formatting goes through `sdscatvprintf()`, the same call
`addReplyErrorFormatInternal()` uses, so `"%f"` renders identically (`invalid
longitude,latitude pair 200.000000,37.000000`). LuaJIT still prepends its usual
`file:line: ` position to the raised message, like any other Lua error; that is
the one difference from a RESP client, and `errtext()` in the smoke test strips
it.

Two texts are the facade's own, because Redis has no equivalent failure:
`"string or number expected, got %s"` (a Lua argument can be a table or a
handle, a RESP argument cannot), `"string expected, got %s"` (the same, for
`load()`, where a number must not be coerced because the argument is a byte
string), and the `luaL_checkstring()` "bad argument" message that option words
produce when handed a non-string. Upstream cannot express any of them -- every
element of `c->argv` is already a string there -- so these read as Lua errors
with the generic code in front of them.

That claim is checked, not asserted: a scan of the facades' 113 `rl_error(L,
"...")` sites against `$RSRC` finds 111 whose literal appears verbatim in the
pristine tree (allowing for the `\r\n` that the `shared.*` error objects carry),
and the 2 remaining ones are the sentences above. The PFCOUNT arity message goes
through `'%s' command` with the name passed as an argument for exactly that
reason: no facade literal is a paraphrase of a Redis message.
